%U A342353 6976032,8771896,10923088,13480000,16495416,20031936,24158528,28946720,34478264,40837920,48111136,56396344,65798728,76425264
%N A342353 Number of ways to choose 3 points that form an equilateral triangle from the A001845(n) points in a regular octahedral grid of size n.
%C A342353 The octahedral grid of side length n is the set of points (x,y,z) in Z^3 such that |x|+|y|+|z| <= n. The number of points in this grid is given by A001845(n).
%C A342353 For an offset pair (v,w) the triangle with vertices 0, v, w is equilateral iff |w|^2 = |v|^2 = |w-v|^2, and the last equality expands to 2*(w.v) = |v|^2. Grouping candidate offsets by squared norm and filtering on that dot product replaces the search over triples of grid points. Going further, the number of offset pairs that pass grows like n^2 while the number of point pairs grows like n^6, so it is faster to loop over triangle shapes and count, for each shape, the translations that fit inside the octahedron; each triangle is then counted by all 6 orderings of its vertices. - _Alper Ferudun_, Jul 29 2026
%H A342353 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A342353/b342353.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..258</a>
%H A342353 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A342353/a342353.txt">C++ program</a>
%e A342353 For n = 1, the a(1) = 8 equilateral triangles are given by the convex hulls of {(+-1,0,0),(0,+-1,0),(0,0,+-1)}.
%o A342353 (C++) // See Links
%Y A342353 Cf. A001845.
%Y A342353 Cf. A102698 and A334581 are analogous for the cubic grid and tetrahedral grid respectively.
%K A342353 nonn,changed
